      "implication": "# 20. But Unknown Does Not Equal Opportunity\\n\\nThis is the most important conclusion.\\n\\nWe have now demonstrated:\\n\\n\`\`\`text\\nBMC ALREADY HAS\\n───────────────\\nERROR CLASSIFICATION\\n+\\nERROR DETAILS\\n+\\nRECOVERY ELIGIBILITY\\n+\\nDEPENDENCY CHECKS\\n+\\nAPPROVAL CONTROLS\\n+\\nCLARIFICATION\\n+\\nREPROCESS\\n+\\nRESTART\\n+\\nSLA VISIBILITY\\n+\\nSTATUS HISTORY\\n\`\`\`\\n\\nTherefore the remaining theoretical ART capability:\\n\\n\`\`\`text\\nDYNAMIC RECOVERY ARBITRATION\\n\`\`\`\\n\\nmay be technically interesting.\\n\\nBut we still lack evidence that:\\n\\n1. enterprises repeatedly face this decision;\\n2. the decision consumes substantial human effort;\\n3. current BMC workflows fail materially at it;\\n4. customers would pay for an external intelligence layer;\\n5. ART can solve it better enough to justify integration.\\n\\n---",
