      "statement": "# 30. Final Verdict\\n\\nOPP-02-03 produced a significant improvement in evidence quality.\\n\\nWe now know BMC's service-request exception architecture is not simplistic.\\n\\nIt supports:\\n\\n\`\`\`text\\nREQUEST\\n ↓\\nMULTIPLE FULFILLMENT OBJECTS\\n ↓\\nCONDITIONAL EXECUTION\\n ↓\\nCROSS-APPLICATION STATUS\\n ↓\\nFAILURE\\n ↓\\nERROR DIAGNOSIS\\n ↓\\nREPROCESS / RESTART\\n ↓\\nELIGIBILITY CHECK\\n ↓\\nVERIFY\\n\`\`\`\\n\\nBMC also documents concrete error-resolution scenarios where operators inspect an error, correct the underlying condition and reprocess the failed record.\\n\\nTherefore the opportunity cannot be generic exception automation.\\n\\nThe only surviving question is:\\n\\n> **When several recovery paths are possible, is there a recurring high-value decision requiring contextual arbitration that BMC's predefined workflows do not adequately solve?**\\n\\nThat question is still open.",
